WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.292
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.292 --> 00:00:08.000
CARTER MORGAN: Welcome
to another episode

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.580
of "VME End to End," a show
where you have someone that's

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:12.590
a little skeptical of
VMs and someone that's

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:15.560
a little more enthusiastic
about them on the show

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:16.880
to hash out all things VM.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:18.890
Brian, thanks so much
for being here today.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:20.330
I've got a tricky one for you.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:22.197
BRIAN DORSEY: OK, let's go.

00:00:22.197 --> 00:00:23.030
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah.

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:25.685
Yeah, I want to know about
cutting edge technology,

00:00:25.685 --> 00:00:28.852
like really pushing cloud
compute machines to the limit.

00:00:28.852 --> 00:00:31.310
I think this is going to be
one that's going to trip us up.

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:33.650
BRIAN DORSEY: I have the
perfect person for us

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:35.360
to bring in and talk
to you about this.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.240
I would like to introduce Emma.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:39.050
Welcome, Emma.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:40.410
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Hi.

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.343
Thank you for having me today.

00:00:42.343 --> 00:00:44.760
CARTER MORGAN: Yeah, so happy
to be able to talk with you.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.810
If I'm not mistaken, you
made an amazing world record

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:52.570
breaking pi calculator using
Google Cloud and Compute, yes?

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:54.480
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
Yes, it was in 2019.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:55.650
So it's a little bit old.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.210
But it was a world record.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.490
CARTER MORGAN: Yes, can
we get an overview of that

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:00.570
project a little bit.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:01.470
What was it?

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:05.129
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Sure, so there
are competitions around pi.

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:09.150
The number begins
with 3.14 and people

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.740
try to calculate as
many digits as possible.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:14.940
And world record
we did was three--

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:21.120
no, 31.4 trillion digits on
Google Cloud, using 25 machines

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:27.030
and spending 121 days and
100 terabytes of storage.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:28.260
And we did that.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.470
BRIAN DORSEY: But this scale is
why I wanted to invite Emma in,

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:36.150
because there's not a lot of
people that have run machines

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:40.000
full on for four
months straight,

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.770
and I think more interestingly,
kind of worked through where

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.170
the bottlenecks are in a
system when you try to do that.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.390
Where were the bottlenecks
in this process?

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:52.060
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Sure, so the
bottleneck was the storage.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.930
So in order to calculate
pi, you need a lot

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.820
of storage, 107 terabytes.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:01.470
But the amount you read
and write is enormous.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.440
We wrote about nine
petabytes, I'm sorry,

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.470
eight petabytes and
read nine petabytes.

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.190
A petabyte is 1,000 terabytes.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:12.900
So it's approximately
17,000 terabytes

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.970
of data you need to process.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:18.185
And the storage I/O was
the biggest bottleneck.

00:02:18.185 --> 00:02:19.560
CARTER MORGAN:
This is one-- this

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.780
is mind breaking to
me, because I would not

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:24.120
expect Cloud Compute to
be able to handle that.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.050
Like that was my assumption
coming into this.

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:28.350
Was this one of the first
projects of its type

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:31.862
to do this, or is this like
commonplace in the cloud?

00:02:31.862 --> 00:02:34.320
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: I think it's
getting more and more common

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.100
these days.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.490
A lot of people just run
high performance computing,

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:41.910
these type of massive
workloads on cloud.

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:43.410
CARTER MORGAN: So
then, my question

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:47.460
would be, what are some
of the advantages of doing

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:48.300
this in the cloud?

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.083
Because if I did this on
prem, I kind of understand

00:02:51.083 --> 00:02:52.500
what that looks
like a little bit.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:55.058
I'd buy a lot of my
machines up front.

00:02:55.058 --> 00:02:56.850
I would try and work
through it from there.

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.350
How does it look
like for the cloud?

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.870
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: So I think
the coolest part of using cloud

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:04.980
is you don't need to
choose which machine

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.410
or which architecture
to use up front.

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:11.430
So, for example, if you're
buying a physical machine

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:14.280
you actually need to decide
how many cores you're

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:18.060
getting, how much
storage you're assigning

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.500
to that machine, et
cetera, et cetera.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:24.690
But with the cloud, you
can always try and test

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:25.650
different parameters.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:29.020
For example, do I
need 100 gigabytes

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:30.630
of memory or 200 gigabytes?

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:33.720
Or do I need 64
cores, 128 cores?

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.810
You can try and test
these parameters

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:39.850
in the real environment.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.210
BRIAN DORSEY: So you basically
do small versions of it

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:44.130
and kind of see
how much work you

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.890
get for a certain amount
of time and money,

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.110
and see how that turns out?

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:53.070
CARTER MORGAN: Wow,
this is really cool.

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:54.660
Something I'm
really curious about

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.390
is, OK, you said
this was in 2019

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:59.580
and it's not the new
record now, or whatever.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:02.740
But what would be different if
you were coding the same thing?

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:06.240
Say you were trying to do 32
trillion digits of pi again.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.650
What would change if you
took that same code base now

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.090
and just, I don't
know, could you

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.880
update it for some of the
compute technology now,

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:15.390
or what?

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:17.160
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
Yeah, so with cloud

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.110
you can just change
the parameters

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:24.090
and use the latest CPUs
and machines we have today.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:28.050
For example, we have the latest
Intel and AMD processors.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:30.240
We have more memory per machine.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.220
For example, the
biggest machine we have

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.770
is 11 terabytes of memory.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:39.750
And we have newer
persistent disk types

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.750
that achieve lower cost
and higher throughput.

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:45.960
And we have increased
the network bandwidth

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:49.450
from 16 gigabits per second
to 100 gigabits per second.

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:52.800
So by using all
of these, I think

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.280
we can probably finish the
calculation in a quarter

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.370
of the time we did in 2019.

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:00.870
BRIAN DORSEY: OK, there's a
kind of a saying, time is money.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:04.740
And at some level that's just
even more true in cloud, right?

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.400
So if you make something
faster it costs less to run.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:08.550
So that's a big deal.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:11.260
CARTER MORGAN: Can we actually
talk about that a little bit?

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:14.310
What was like, say, the
cost of this in 2019?

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:17.640
And then you said it's
four times faster?

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:20.730
What would the cost of it
look like now, just estimates?

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:24.390
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Sure, so
if you don't work for Google,

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:26.880
you'd probably pay a
lot for this project.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:31.770
And it was about
$300,000 to calculate pi,

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:36.120
if you paid the money,
as an external customer.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:43.860
And you get half the
storage for half the price

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:46.210
today, achieve the
same bandwidth.

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:50.040
So that cut the cost about
30%, because the biggest cost

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:50.880
was the storage.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:53.280
And we had the faster processor.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:58.170
We can use the Intel Ice
Lake Xeon processors.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:01.420
And we have increased
the network bandwidth.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:05.307
So in combined, it
took about four months

00:06:05.307 --> 00:06:06.390
to finish the calculation.

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:12.510
I think we can do something
between a month or probably

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:14.260
40-50 days.

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:18.150
So you pay less part time.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:21.060
Like you pay less per hour,
and you finish the calculation

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:21.670
faster.

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:24.240
So by combining all
of these factors,

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.402
I think it's safe to say we'd
pay much, much less today.

00:06:28.402 --> 00:06:29.610
BRIAN DORSEY: That's awesome.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:34.620
So like same project over
time gets cheaper and cheaper.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:36.070
That seems like a win.

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:38.195
But it seems like you also
did a lot of-- you know,

00:06:38.195 --> 00:06:40.980
like there's a lot of variations
for how things could be done.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:45.150
You talked about running
experiments, basically.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:47.520
If people wanted to run
their own experiments

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.020
on their own software,
how would you

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:50.790
recommend they approach that?

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:52.650
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
Sure, so there are

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:56.190
tons of configurations
and variables in cloud,

00:06:56.190 --> 00:07:00.540
and probably with your software
you want to run in cloud.

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:04.620
And, of course, you can
automate some of the aspects

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:05.760
for your software.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:10.150
And there is a tool to automate
that for cloud as well.

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:12.330
It's called
Infrastructure as a Code.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:16.470
And we use tools like
Terraform and Pulumi

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:21.450
to actually write down all
the configuration parameters

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:24.780
as a script, and run it.

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:30.600
And the coolest part is you
can automate the deployment

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.650
and provisioning process.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:35.880
So for example, if you want to
test with different CPU cores

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:42.630
like 2, 4, 8, 16,
32, 64, then you just

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:45.900
need to write a for loop and
run the script again and again,

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:49.510
and invoke the software,
and run the test for all

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:50.700
the combinations.

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:52.470
And that's what you
can do with the cloud.

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:53.262
CARTER MORGAN: Wow.

00:07:53.262 --> 00:07:56.040
Yeah, like I can
totally see, again, I'm

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:57.120
still a little skeptical.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:59.260
I'm blown away that
this is possible.

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:01.530
But I see the benefit of
being able to just say

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:03.690
let me try out all these
different configurations

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:05.440
for my workload.

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:08.588
And so I'm curious, what are
some other things that you

00:08:08.588 --> 00:08:11.130
think are pretty cool about this
type of hardware, that might

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:13.350
not be obvious to
someone like me who

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:15.492
doesn't do this kind of
stuff as deeply as you do?

00:08:15.492 --> 00:08:16.950
EMMA HARUKA IWAO:
Right, it's good.

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:20.010
You may think, hey, I
can get the same hardware

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:22.740
from a hardware store
or hardware vendor.

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:27.120
But it's actually not--
well, it's complicated,

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:29.510
because you buy hardware, right?

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:33.970
You buy a new machine and you
use it for three, four years,

00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:34.470
right?

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:40.230
You don't need, you don't
buy a new server every month.

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:43.950
With the cloud, you just need
to shut down the machine,

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:45.810
change the configuration,
and reboot.

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:48.420
And then you get access
to the latest hardware

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:50.380
without paying any upfront cost.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:56.700
So if you want to, say, use the
latest Xeon processor, or SSD,

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:00.245
or NVMe, or AMD
Milan, then you just

00:09:00.245 --> 00:09:01.620
need to change
the configuration.

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:05.580
And then you keep all the
data, all the configurations,

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:10.170
and just get the latest
hardware for like free,

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:11.550
without any upfront cost.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:17.030
So I think that's pretty cool.

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:20.780
Like you can actually
use the latest hardware

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:21.590
anytime on cloud.

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:25.790
And actually cloud
is one of the first,

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:28.940
earliest places to
support such, like,

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:31.110
latest generations of hardware.

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:33.110
CARTER MORGAN: Oh, man,
that killed my argument,

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:34.860
because I was just
going to be like, well,

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:37.220
why don't I just buy
the newest technology?

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:39.575
But a lot of times it
doesn't roll out as fast

00:09:39.575 --> 00:09:40.853
like as it would to the cloud.

00:09:40.853 --> 00:09:42.770
BRIAN DORSEY: And you
mentioned the networking

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:43.730
got a lot faster?

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:47.980
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Yeah,
so network is faster.

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:52.240
Obviously, you have the
ethernet and the switch

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:55.480
and on top of rack switch
and the whole back end.

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:58.600
And I think the
cool part of cloud

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:02.800
is you don't need to think
about the actual fabric.

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:08.240
So we support 100 gigabits
per second for network.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:11.500
And each machine can
actually communicate

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:14.680
to another machine with
100 gigabits per second,

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:16.660
regardless of their
physical position.

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:21.610
So like when you're actually
designing a physical data

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:26.080
center, you actually need
to think of those top rack

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:28.600
bandwidth and how you
distribute workloads,

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:31.420
so you get the full
bandwidth for each machine.

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:34.990
But with cloud, we design
the network in a way

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:38.410
that each machine can actually
achieve the full bisection

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:39.780
of bandwidth.

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:41.530
BRIAN DORSEY: OK, so
that's a whole thing,

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:43.613
like from an architecture
and planning standpoint,

00:10:43.613 --> 00:10:46.687
you just don't even have to
worry about it, think about it.

00:10:46.687 --> 00:10:48.520
CARTER MORGAN: This is
so interesting to me.

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:50.740
One thing I'm curious
about is if you

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:53.428
were going to do a project
like this in the future,

00:10:53.428 --> 00:10:55.720
like if you were going to
try and go for another record

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:58.220
or something like that, would
you still use cloud computers,

00:10:58.220 --> 00:11:00.940
or do you think you might switch
over and use something on prem,

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:03.343
something more dedicated?

00:11:03.343 --> 00:11:05.260
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: I,
because I work for cloud,

00:11:05.260 --> 00:11:11.350
I would use, obviously, there
are obviously pros and cons.

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:16.450
For example, if you want to
use a specific hardware set

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:19.900
or specific, for
example, if you want

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:22.060
to test a specific
piece of hardware,

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:24.760
then you might need
to still get access

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:27.400
to that specialty equipment.

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:29.680
But for like, I
think, I would say

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:32.920
99% of the workload
in the world,

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:35.770
you can just launch
a machine in cloud.

00:11:35.770 --> 00:11:41.380
And because you don't know which
machine to buy before actually

00:11:41.380 --> 00:11:42.560
getting the machine.

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:46.660
So for on premise hardware,
you kind of do the guesswork.

00:11:46.660 --> 00:11:51.370
And I think this hardware
with this number of cores

00:11:51.370 --> 00:11:55.210
and this much memory
sufficiently big enough,

00:11:55.210 --> 00:12:00.520
and not too big, but
in sometimes use,

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:02.680
like you don't
have enough memory,

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:04.300
or you don't have
enough storage,

00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:08.230
or you may need
to add some cores.

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:09.520
You never know.

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:16.320
And with the cloud you can
just test everything, right?

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:18.240
And you can always
change the configuration.

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:21.060
You can always add new hardware.

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:26.172
And, yeah, so you don't
need to choose and invest

00:12:26.172 --> 00:12:26.880
at the beginning.

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:31.000
You just change and
adapt along the way.

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:33.000
BRIAN DORSEY: This is a
really interesting angle

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:36.280
on, people often say, if you're
kind of doing a batch workload,

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:38.040
then maybe that's
not the sweet spot.

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:42.143
But there's this up-front
like testing side of it,

00:12:42.143 --> 00:12:43.560
where you're being
more scientific

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:46.070
